  "narrative": "I reassessed the newly delivered discovery results with the fixed scientific criterion that a clinically meaningful dependency-score contrast should be around or above a 10% relative difference; using the approximate comparator means, this is about 0.024 units for WRN, EGFR, ERBB2, KRAS, POLR2A, TMED10, DHX9, KIF18A, RPL11, and STAT3, about 0.041 units for SMARCA2, and smaller for NFE2L2/USP7. The four Avana-library quality-control contrasts are exploratory multiplicity-prone checks for possible technical confounding of the accepted strong effects. H27 showed a WRN Avana association of about 0.022 units, just below the approximate 10% scale; its interval crossed zero and included zero as a plausible value, and the non-Avana cell was relatively small. I rejected it as unsupported rather than accepting a clinically meaningful technical association, although it leaves residual uncertainty about modest library effects. H28, H29, and H30 did not support the hypothesized Avana direction: H28 was opposite in SMARCA2, H30 was opposite in USP7, and H29 in NFE2L2 was too imprecise and not replicated as a meaningful library effect. These do not threaten the accepted very large biological associations, but they flag that small technical associations should not be overinterpreted. The interaction tests are negative: H31 does not establish that the accepted EGFR-amplification/KRAS-wt adenocarcinoma effect is uniquely subgroup-specific; H32 does not support lineage modification of the huge WRN/MSI-high association; H33 is an underpowered interaction of the strong SMARCA4-loss/SMARCA2 association with ARID1A loss, with a point estimate below the SMARCA2 threshold and an interval spanning zero; and H34 does not support lineage modification of the strong USP7/TP53 association. All four are rejected and closed as unsupported specificity or modification claims. In contrast, the stratum-robustness analyses strongly support the core dataset-specific associations. H40 shows WRN dependency remains very strongly associated with MSI-high within TP53-wild-type cells. H42 shows the USP7 TP53-wild-type versus TP53-loss contrast remains very strong in MYC-wild-type cells. H44 shows the SMARCA2 association with SMARCA4 loss remains very strong in ARID1A-wild-type cells. These intervals are far from zero and far beyond the clinical-magnitude scale, making residual confounding by TP53, MYC amplification, or ARID1A loss unlikely to explain the entire effect, though they do not establish causality. H41 shows the same enormous association between KEAP1 loss and NFE2L2 dependency within TP53-wild-type cells. The display sign for NFE2L2 claims has a residual sign-presentation ambiguity in the ledger because the previously accepted H8 contrast and H41 have large negative displayed estimates while some other accepted effects have large positive estimates; I evaluated H41 by its magnitude and by consistency with the previously accepted NFE2L2/KEAP1-loss contrast rather than treating it as a reversal, and I flag the presentation inconsistency. The effect size is so large that this display ambiguity does not affect the scientific conclusion that KEAP1 loss is strongly associated with a different NFE2L2 dependency state in this dataset. I request independent validation for H8 because it is one of the strongest canonical accepted effects without independent validation, and independent replication would provide a useful check on the KEAP1/NFE2L2 association before further subgroup refinement. All conclusions remain associations in this supplied lung-lineage collection, not causal or generalizable claims.",
